## 11. PRIVACY AND DATA MANAGEMENT

Create a Privacy and Data section.

Users should be able to understand and manage their own financial data.

### Data export

If Phase 6 report exports are implemented, reuse the existing CSV/PDF export infrastructure where appropriate.

If the project supports a broader account-data export, provide a secure export workflow for the user's own data.

Possible data categories include:

- Transactions.
- Budgets.
- Savings goals.
- Contribution history.
- User preferences.

Do not expose another user's data or private authentication fields.

Do not create a duplicate export implementation if the existing report system can be extended safely.

### Data deletion

If account deletion is supported by the authentication provider and application architecture, implement a carefully controlled account-deletion workflow.

Requirements:

- Explain the consequences before deletion.
- Require explicit confirmation.
- Require reauthentication or other appropriate verification.
- Clearly explain whether related financial records will be deleted or retained.
- Handle dependent records according to the application's existing ownership and retention rules.
- Use a safe, transactional deletion process where supported.
- Handle partial failures.
- Prevent a user from deleting another user's account or records.

Do not add an immediate, one-click destructive delete action.

Do not delete or modify the database schema to implement account deletion.

If safe deletion requires additional legal, retention, or infrastructure decisions, provide a clearly marked unavailable state and document the exact blocker.

### Privacy transparency

Display only truthful information about data handling.

Do not invent claims such as “your data is never stored,” “all data is end-to-end encrypted,” or “your data is automatically deleted” unless the actual implementation supports them.

